# Post-change docs

When you **finish** work that touches a **related directory** (the subtree where most changed paths live),
and when the modification creates structural change or change that worth documentation,

- **New or changed public surface** (HTTP/RPC API, proto, CLI flags, env vars, config schema), or
- **Architecture / boundaries** (new module, moved entrypoint, renamed package), or
- **Dependencies or runtime** requirements changed for that area.

**Then** before you call the task done, you MUST scan through documentation directory, which usually notated as `/docs` in path, and conduct documentation update that reflects the current state of project.

If the change is fairly large, such as a change that touches **5+ files**, hand off to the **`/post-change-docs`** subagent (the slash command that runs the post-change-docs workflow) . That subagent owns targeted doc sync for the **current workspace**.

If you cannot trigger slash commands from this session, **tell the user explicitly** to run **`/post-change-docs`** next and give a one-line summary of the subtree and what changed (API, config, etc.) so the subagent has context.

**Skip** when the change is purely internal (private rename, comments-only, formatting) and no doc or contract would be wrong.
